content:
Looking at the evidence, the rollback at t=530 (E11) happened 2 seconds after the first anomaly (t=528), but errors are still at peak 1.95/s at t=590 (E3, E7). The payment-provider is returning 503s (E9) which started at t=528 — before the rollback. This strongly suggests the external payment-provider is the root cause, not the deploy. I need to verify whether the 503s are continuing post-rollback and check the payment-provider's status.

```json
{
  "investigate": [
    {
      "tool": "logs",
      "service": "payment-service",
      "level": "ERROR",
      "contains": "provider request failed",
      "why": "Confirm whether 503 errors from payment-provider are still occurring after the rollback at t=530, to determine if the external dependency is still failing."
    },
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT status, COUNT(*) as cnt, MIN(created_at) as first_seen, MAX(updated_at) as last_seen FROM payments GROUP BY status ORDER BY last_seen DESC",
      "why": "Check the distribution and timing of payment statuses to confirm ongoing failures vs. captured payments post-rollback."
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "service": "payment-service",
      "kind": "external_dependency",
      "mechanism": "The external payment-provider began returning HTTP 503 errors at t=528, causing all payment captures to fail; the rollback to v1.0 did not resolve this because the provider itself is unavailable.",
      "supporting": ["E9", "E3", "E7", "E6", "E1", "E15", "E16"],
      "refuting": ["E11"],
      "explains": ["I7_payments_succeed", "M2_error_burst"],
      "repair_plan": []
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The external payment-provider has been returning 503 errors since t=528 (before the rollback), and errors persist at peak rate post-rollback, indicating the provider itself is down — not a bad deploy."
}
```
